    "evidence_text": "33.33 average of ROUGE-1, ROUGE-2 and ROUGE-L on the CNN/Daily Mail, which is state-of-the-art.\n\nOn the New York Times dataset, our model achieves about 5.6% relative improvement over ROUGE-1.\n\nThe goal of the model is to maximize the probability of generating the correct target sequences.\n\nIn the encoding and generation process, the attention mechanism is used to concentrate on the most important positions of text.\n\n$N$ layer Transformer decoder which is the same as the draft decoder.\n\nIt concentrates on one word at a time, based\n\non the source document as well as other words.\n\nSo in our model all decoders share the same parameters.\n\nResearchers usually use ROUGE as the evaluation metric for summarization, however during sequence-to-sequence model training, the objective is to maximize the log likelihood of generated sequences.\n\nfrom predicted distribution, and $R(a^s)$ is the reward score compared with the ground-truth summary, we use ROUGE-L in our experiment.\n\nIn the refine process we introduce similar objectives.\n\nDuring training we feed the ground-truth summary to each decoder and minimize the objective.\n\nWe use WordPiece embeddings with a 30,000 vocabulary which is the same as BERT.\n\nand the label smoothing value to 0.1.\n\nWe set the RL objective factor $\\gamma $ to 0.99.\n\nWe use beam size 4 and length penalty of 1.0 to generate logical form sequences.\n\nIt is a nice method to avoid phrase repetition since the two datasets seldom contains repeated tri-grams in one summary.\n\nWe also fine tune the generated sequences using another two simple rules.\n\nTo evaluate the performance of our model, we conduct experiments on CNN/Daily Mail dataset, which is a large collection of news\n\nOn NYT50, following  we evaluate limited length ROUGE recall score(limit the generated summary length to the ground truth length).\n\nWe split NYT50 summaries into sentences by semicolons to calculate the ROUGE scores.\n\nWe classify them to two groups based on whether they are extractive or abstractive models.\n\nExcept the four scores, our model outperforms these\n\nWe use three ablation models for the experiments.\n\nThe ablation study shows that each module is necessary for our full model, and the improvements are statistically significant on all metrics.\n\nTo evaluate the impact of summary length on model performance, we compare the average rouge score\n\nimprovements of our model with different length of ground-truth summaries.\n\nAdditional Results on NYT50\tTable 2 shows experiments on the NYT50 corpus.\n\nSo the model needs to catch long-term dependency of the sequences to generate good summaries.\n\nThe first two lines of Table 2 show results of the two baselines introduced by  : these baselines select first\n\nn sentences, or select the first k words from the original document.\n\nOn ROUGE-2, our model also get an improvement of 0.51.\n\nThe experiment proves that our approach can outperform competitive methods on different data distributions.\n\nSome recent works also focus on leveraging pre-trained language models in summarization.\n\npretrain a language model and use it as the sentiment analyser when generating reviews of goods.\n\ntrain a language model on golden summaries, and then use it on the decoder side to incorporate prior knowledge.",
